---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
---

<DocsLayout title="Passwordless Email Login">
  <h1>Passwordless Email Login</h1>
  <p>
    A user can authenticate by proving control of their email — no
    password — via a <strong>6-digit one-time code (OTP)</strong> or a
    clickable <strong>magic link</strong>. Both arms land on the single
    account keyed by that email: a passwordless login for an address that
    already has a password, OAuth, or passkey account links to the
    <em>same</em> user rather than creating a duplicate. First-time emails
    are auto-created on verify, so signup and login are one flow.
  </p>
  <p>
    Accounts are created <strong>only on verify / redeem</strong>, never on
    request, so an attacker cannot manufacture accounts for emails they
    don't control. Auto-create is gated by
    <code>GATEWAY_PASSWORDLESS_SIGNUP_ENABLED</code> (default
    <code>true</code>); when off, an unknown email that proves control
    still cannot log in and the response is indistinguishable from any
    other failure.
  </p>

  <h2>One-time code (OTP)</h2>
  <p>
    <code>RequestEmailLoginCode</code> emails a 6-digit code. The response
    is identical whether or not the email has an account
    (anti-enumeration).
  </p>
  <Code code={`curl -sX POST http://localhost/identity.v1.IdentityService/RequestEmailLoginCode \\
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"email":"alice@example.com"}'`} lang="bash" />
  <p>
    <code>VerifyEmailLoginCode</code> validates the code, resolves-or-creates
    the user, and returns access + refresh tokens.
  </p>
  <Code code={`curl -sX POST http://localhost/identity.v1.IdentityService/VerifyEmailLoginCode \\
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"email":"alice@example.com","code":"123456"}'`} lang="bash" />
  <p>
    The code is single-use and short-lived
    (<code>GATEWAY_PASSWORDLESS_CODE_TTL_SECONDS</code>, default 300). A
    wrong guess increments a per-code attempt counter; after
    <code>GATEWAY_PASSWORDLESS_CODE_MAX_ATTEMPTS</code> (default 5) the code
    is invalidated. A consumed or expired code, a wrong code, and an
    exhausted code all return <code>unauthenticated</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Magic link</h2>
  <p>
    <code>RequestMagicLink</code> emails a single-use sign-in link. The
    <code>return_to</code> is validated against the same
    <code>GATEWAY_OAUTH_ALLOWED_RETURN_URLS</code> allowlist the hosted
    OAuth flow uses; a disallowed <code>return_to</code> is rejected with
    <code>invalid_argument</code>.
  </p>
  <Code code={`curl -sX POST http://localhost/identity.v1.IdentityService/RequestMagicLink \\
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"email":"alice@example.com","returnTo":"https://app.example.com/welcome"}'`} lang="bash" />
  <p>
    <code>RedeemMagicLink</code> consumes the token, resolves-or-creates the
    user, and returns tokens plus the validated <code>return_to</code> the
    SPA should navigate to.
  </p>
  <Code code={`curl -sX POST http://localhost/identity.v1.IdentityService/RedeemMagicLink \\
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"token":"<token-from-email-link>"}'`} lang="bash" />
  <p>
    The token is high-entropy, single-use, and short-lived
    (<code>GATEWAY_PASSWORDLESS_MAGIC_LINK_TTL_SECONDS</code>, default 900).
    A replay or an expired token returns <code>unauthenticated</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Spam / abuse controls</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Control</th><th>Knob</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Per-IP rate limit on both Request endpoints</td><td><code>GATEWAY_RATE_LIMIT_PASSWORDLESS_PER_IP</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Per-email send cooldown (one inbox can't be flooded)</td><td><code>GATEWAY_EMAIL_SEND_COOLDOWN_SECONDS</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>OTP brute-force cap</td><td><code>GATEWAY_PASSWORDLESS_CODE_MAX_ATTEMPTS</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>OTP TTL</td><td><code>GATEWAY_PASSWORDLESS_CODE_TTL_SECONDS</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Magic-link TTL</td><td><code>GATEWAY_PASSWORDLESS_MAGIC_LINK_TTL_SECONDS</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Single-use replay rejection</td><td>built in (consumed-at compare-and-set)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Anti-enumeration on Request responses</td><td>built in</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>
    Current defaults for each knob are in the
    <a href="/identity/docs/installation/configuration#passwordless-email-login">configuration
    reference</a> (the Passwordless and Rate-limiting sections).
  </p>

  <h2>Unified accounts</h2>
  <p>
    OAuth, OTP, and magic link all converge on one account per email via a
    shared <code>resolveOrCreateUserByEmail</code> helper. Signing up with a
    password and later logging in passwordlessly returns the same user id —
    no duplicate is created. Proving control of the inbox also marks the
    email verified.
  </p>

  <h2>How auto-created users map to tenants</h2>
  <p>
    A passwordless login resolves the identity by
    <code>(project_id, email)</code> within the request's
    <a href="/identity/docs/concepts/multi-tenancy">Project</a>. An auto-created user is
    a single identity in that Project's user pool — one human is one user per
    Project, regardless of how many company domains they touch. The
    <a href="/identity/docs/concepts/multi-tenancy">Tenant</a> is derived afterward from
    the verified email domain, not chosen at login.
  </p>
  <p>
    Auto-create only establishes the identity; it does not grant tenant
    membership. A user who proves control of <code>alice@acme.com</code> can
    authenticate, but tenant-scoped access still requires materialized
    membership of the <code>acme</code> tenant (via a verified domain or an
    accepted <a href="/identity/docs/auth/invitations">invitation</a>).
  </p>

  <h2>Disabling auto-create</h2>
  <p>
    Set <code>GATEWAY_PASSWORDLESS_SIGNUP_ENABLED=false</code> to require a
    pre-existing account: an unknown email that proves control is rejected
    with the same <code>unauthenticated</code> shape every other failure
    returns, so the endpoint never reveals which addresses exist. Existing
    accounts still log in passwordlessly.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
